Dear Natasha,

&nbsp;

Welcome back to PG after your maternity leave. I hope you and the family are adjusting well.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

Thank you for your email. I am disappointed to note the position taken by the company given my relentless +6 years worth of service to the firm and given I remain one of the longest standing employees.
 I also note that I am hopeful of returning to work prior to 15 December. In the event this is possible, can you please let me know who I should contact and how to have my system access restored?

&nbsp;

In terms of the eligibility, can you point me to where in my contract it states that to be eligible for full pay (termed OSP in your below email) I have not given notice of termination of my employment?
 For the avoidance of doubt, it does not stand to reason that simply due to my resignation, that in of itself does not constitute non-compliance with the terms of the Agreement.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

Further, can you advise if the same eligibility criteria (and the same resultant decision to not permit full pay in exception to 13.4 of my contract) have been applied to other members of staff who have
 been required to take a period of sickness during their notice period?&nbsp;

&nbsp;

Regarding my final pay, and in the event I am entitled to receive full pay for my absence (subject to the above), I believe my final pay should consist of:

&nbsp;


Full pay from 1 December to 12 December (anticipated as full pay under 13.4 of my contract)
Any full pay for days which I return to full work;
Outstanding annual leave of 6 days; and
11 days worth of my acting up allowance (£3,175 gross). The 1 September letter from Raminder confirms that this will be effective from 1 September 2025 and will be reviewed on a monthly basis. It further states
 that any changes to this will be discussed in advance and confirmed in writing. I have received no such notice and so this is still due paid for this month.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

