ned with what is said about the Gramercy additional funding in Alicia Alinia’s recent First Witness Statement. Particularly she says the 30m additional is reserved to only be spent on these proceedings while the 65m is also for wider overheads. We need to be consistent here.@Neil Pigott ?&nbsp;Are there any other funders of the litigation, including historic, who might be said to contribute to the Claimant’s costs? I distantly recall I have seen mention of another funder - but is that historic?We said the below in Tom’s March witness statement, this would have been in relation to NorthWall. &nbsp;The funds provided by Gramercy were used, in part, to repay previous debt finance provided to Pogust Goodhead, which has been superseded by the funding.&nbsp;We have not disclosed the $140m G2 facility which is exhausted and did not provide funding for the Nox litigation (save for overheads in the same way as G3)&nbsp;Other potentially relevant funders which we discussed yesterday include Locke, Fulcrum, DRS, and Pogust Goodhead LLC, who provided funding for Nox client aggregation costs (marketing) &nbsp;&nbsp;Tom&nbsp;&nbsp;​​​​Tom&nbsp;CrowGroup&nbsp;Financial&nbsp;Controller+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7890&nbsp;043&nbsp;708This email and any attachments are confidential and may also be privileged. If you receive this message in error, please contact the sender immediately, destroy the email and any attachments and do not use, copy, store or disclose this email and any attachments for any purpose. Pogust Goodhead does not accept service of documents by electronic means without express prior agreement.&nbsp;Pogust Goodhead is the trading name of&nbsp;PGMBM Law Ltd, a&nbsp;limited liability company registered in England and Wales (no. 6284764) with its&nbsp;registered office at&nbsp;53-64 Chancery House, Chancery Lane, London WC2A 1QS&nbsp;and authorised and regulated by the Solicitors' Regulation Authority. We use the term ‘partner’ to refer to an employee or consultant of Pogust Go