.com&gt;; Michael Burke &lt;mburke@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;spopplewell@leighday.co.uk &lt;spopplewell@leighday.co.uk&gt;; Jamie Macfarlane &lt;jmacfarlane@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; KLatham@kingschambers.com &lt;KLatham@kingschambers.com&gt;; T.Barclay@4newsquare.com &lt;T.Barclay@4newsquare.com&gt;; B.Williams@4newsquare.com &lt;B.Williams@4newsquare.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;Re: Defendants’ Submissions and Draft Order – Costs of CMH2 &nbsp;Hi Simon,Thanks – and noted re: adding 60% to the Note.On the post-July issue:I’ll need to defer to @Michael Burke&nbsp;and @Fiona Huddleston&nbsp;whether there are realistic scenarios where Claimants could receive a judgment in the PDD trial that is effectively adverse but does not result in the T3 trial being vacated. I appreciate your caution that the position may not be quite so black and white, and it would be helpful to get a view from the PG/LD legal teams on that before you finalise the Note.I take your point on the PTR being likely to be incurred by end of July. Likewise, I agree that there may well be elements of Trial Prep approved costs (e.g. brief fees, trial bundles, initial solicitor work) incurred before the end of July, even if the trial is ultimately vacated.&nbsp;I'm away for the rest of the week, but aside from ironing out point 1 above, the note is otherwise good to go.Thanks again,Sarah&nbsp;​​​​Sarah&nbsp;BinghamLegal&nbsp;Director+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7484&nbsp;085&nbsp;187From:&nbsp;Simon Teasdale &lt;S.Teasdale@4newsquare.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;Monday, October 06, 2025 11:18To:&nbsp;Sarah Bingham &lt;sbingham@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Sophia Thomson &lt;sthomson@leighday.co.uk&gt;; Fiona Huddleston &lt;FHuddleston@leighday.co.uk&gt;; Anna Varga &lt;avarga@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Michael Burke &lt;mburke@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;spopplewell@leighday.co.uk &lt;spopplewell@leighday.co.uk&gt;; Jamie Macfarlane &lt;jmacfarlane@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; KLatham@kingschambers.com &lt;KLatham@kingschambers.com&gt;; T.Barclay@4newsquare.com &lt;T.Barclay@4newsquare.com&gt;; B.Williams@4newsquare.com &lt;B.Williams@4newsquare.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;RE: Defendants’ Submissions and Draft Order – Costs of CMH2&nbsp;CAUTION: This email originated from outside of the organization. Do not click links or open attachments unless you recognize the sender and know the content is safe.&nbsp;Thanks Sarah, I’ll adjust to take account of that point about % of incurred costs (but will wait for any other comments before doing so).&nbsp;On the Post-July point:We are 100% on the same page for the scenario if Cs win the PDD trial.On scenario B (Cs lose PDD), I think the difference come from these two parts of your list below:Quantum trial will not happen&nbsp;– when we were discussing this earlier in the year pre-PTR, I recall there was hesitation in the group about whether it would be as simple as this (or at least whether it was something we were happy to express this in these absolute terms). That’s where my concerns about the case for ‘full’ cover originate from. If we are now able (and happy) to say that an adverse outcome in the PDD trial would mean in practice that the T3 trial was immediately vacated that’s fine, but my understanding from previous chats was that it might not be as simple as this, and/or there could be outcomes which were effectively losses but which were not strictly fatal to the T3 trial going ahead? If we can’t assert that T3 would go away immediately upon an adverse PDD judgment, then we can’t say the costs will either, and so that’s where the difference of approach arises from I think.Quantum PTR, Trial Prep and Trial Phases will ever be incurred&nbsp;– if the T3 trial does get vacated at the end of July 2026, then you are correct that the Trial phase shouldn’t be incurred (unless some small part is used for a form of ‘consequentials’ but:The PTR is due to be held in July 2026 I think ([47] of Quantum directions) so there’s a very real chance those costs are incurred, even if the hearing itself gets used for something else at the last minute; andGiven the trial starts in October with summer in between, I anticipate at least some of the trial prep costs will be incurred before the end of July. I expect some tranches of brief will be incurred, and there will also be at least some solicitor work (eg I can see there are trial bundle deadlines in July at [52]-[55]).So, I think the key question is whether or not we can say with confidence that if Cs lose (/ receive an adverse costs order in) T2, that is likely to mean that the T3 trial gets abandoned at the end of July 2026. I’m in PG/LDs hands on that. If that’s something we can say, then yes, I can see the argument that there is unlikely to be a set of circumstances which lead to needing cover for the ‘full’ T3 period. If it is more complicated that that, T3 might still go ahead, that’s where we get the potential for a greater today ATE requirement.But even if it is correct and the T3 trial would get immediately vacated, I don’t think it would necessarily mean that none of Quantum PTR or Trial Prep are incurred. I expect at least some of each would be incurred (and TP is obviously a sizeable sum).&nbsp;ThanksSimon&nbsp;&nbsp;Simon&nbsp;Teasdale​​​​BarristerDirect&nbsp;+442078222119&nbsp;Mobile&nbsp;+447969074414&nbsp;Chambers&nbsp;+442078222000&nbsp;4&nbsp;New&nbsp;Square&nbsp;Chambers​Lincoln's&nbsp;Inn,&nbsp;London&nbsp;WC2A&nbsp;3RJFollow&nbsp;us&nbsp;on&nbsp;YouTube | LinkedIn​Discover&nbsp;our Events&nbsp;Privilege and Confidentiality Notice:This email is private as between the sender and the intended recipient. The information contained in this message and any file that may be attached to it are confidential between those parties and may be privileged. Any other party who receives it in error is not permitted to read, use, copy, discuss or pass on the information contained in either the message or its attachments, nor are they entitled to take any action in reliance on the contents.